Line-by-Line Breakdown of the Overpass Query

    [out:json][timeout:60];: Instructs the Overpass API to return the results in JSON format and sets a server-side execution timeout limit of 60 seconds.

    [bbox:42.75,-79.05,43.05,-78.65];: Defines the geographic bounding box using coordinates in the order (South, West, North, East) covering the wider Buffalo, NY area.

    ( and ): Opens and closes a union block, combining the results of all individual filter statements inside.

    nwr["office"][name];: Searches for nodes (n), ways (w), and relations (r) tagged with office that also have a name tag.

    nwr["shop"][name];: Searches for nodes, ways, and relations tagged with shop that have a name.

    nwr["industrial"][name];: Searches for industrial areas or features with a name.

    nwr["craft"][name];: Searches for craft businesses (e.g., workshops, trades) with a name.

    nwr["amenity"~"bank|clinic|doctors|dentist"][name];: Uses a regular expression to search for specific amenities (banks, clinics, doctors, or dentists) that possess a name.

    ;: Terminates individual query statements.

    out center tags;: Outputs the results, calculating a geographic center point for ways and relations (such as buildings or land polygons) and including all of their OpenStreetMap tags.

Expected Output Structure

The output will be formatted as a single JSON file containing a root object with metadata (such as the API version and generation timestamp) and an array called elements. Each item in the array represents a matched business or location with its ID, type, geographic coordinates (latitude and longitude or center coordinates), and a dictionary of its OSM tags.

Depending on the urban density of the Buffalo bounding box, the resulting file will typically range from several megabytes to tens of megabytes, containing thousands of individual JSON objects.
Setting Up and Running in an Incus System Container

Because you are running a Fedora Atomic host with Incus, you can spin up a clean, isolated system container to handle the query execution without modifying your immutable host environment.

1. Launch and enter an Alpine-based container:
Bash

incus launch images:alpine/3.21/cloud overpass-runner
incus exec overpass-runner -- sh

2. Install curl inside the container:
Bash

apk add curl

3. Create the query file:
Write your query to a file named query.overpass:
Bash

cat << 'EOF' > query.overpass
[out:json][timeout:60];
[bbox:42.75,-79.05,43.05,-78.65];
(
  nwr["office"][name];
  nwr["shop"][name];
  nwr["industrial"][name];
  nwr["craft"][name];
  nwr["amenity"~"bank|clinic|doctors|dentist"][name];
);
out center tags;
EOF

4. Execute the query against the Overpass API:
Bash

curl -X POST --data @query.overpass https://overpass-api.de/api/interpreter -o buffalo_businesses.json

5. Transfer the file back to your host:
Once the query completes, you can pull the resulting JSON file directly from the container to your Fedora host machine:
Bash

incus file pull overpass-runner/root/buffalo_businesses.json ./buffalo_businesses.json

